old text-foreground mb-3\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Popular Salary Calculations\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-4\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Quick access to take-home pay estimates for common salary levels.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"flex flex-wrap gap-3\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L11\\\",\\\"/salary/50000\\\",{\\\"href\\\":\\\"/salary/50000\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-emerald-500/20 bg-emerald-500/5 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-foreground hover:border-emerald-500/40 hover:bg-emerald-500/10 transition-colors\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"$$50,000\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L11\\\",\\\"/salary/75000\\\",{\\\"href\\\":\\\"/salary/75000\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-emerald-500/20 bg-emerald-500/5 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-foreground hover:border-emerald-500/40 hover:bg-emerald-500/10 transition-colors\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"$$75,000\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L11\\\",\\\"/salary/100000\\\",{\\\"href\\\":\\\"/salary/100000\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-emerald-500/20 bg-emerald-500/5 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-foreground hover:border-emerald-500/40 hover:bg-emerald-500/10 transition-colors\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"$$100,000\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L11\\\",\\\"/salary/150000\\\",{\\\"href\\\":\\\"/salary/150000\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-emerald-500/20 bg-emerald-500/5 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-foreground hover:border-emerald-500/40 hover:bg-emerald-500/10 transition-colors\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"$$150,000\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L11\\\",\\\"/salary/200000\\\",{\\\"href\\\":\\\"/salary/200000\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-emerald-500/20 bg-emerald-500/5 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-foreground hover:border-emerald-500/40 hover:bg-emerald-500/10 transition-colors\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"$$200,000\\\"}]]}]]}]\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"26:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"section\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mb-8\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"h2\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"text-lg font-bold text-foreground mb-4\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Related Articles\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L11\\\",\\\"california-tax-guide-2026\\\",{\\\"href\\\":\\\"/blog/california-tax-guide-2026\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"group rounded-xl border border-border/50 bg-card p-5 hover:border-emerald-500/30 hover:shadow-md transition-all\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"flex items-center gap-2 mb-2\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border px-2 py-0.5 text-[10px] font-medium bg-violet-100 text-violet-800 dark:bg-violet-500/20 dark:text-violet-400 border-violet-300 dark:border-violet-500/30\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"State Tax\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"text-[10px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"June 19, 2026\\\"}]]}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"h3\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"text-sm font-semibold text-foreground group-hover:text-emerald-600 dark:group-hover:text-emerald-400 transition-colors line-clamp-2 mb-1.5\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"California Tax Guide 2026: Income Tax, Sales Tax, and Local Rates\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"text-xs text-muted-foreground line-clamp-2\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Complete guide to California taxes in 2026. Income tax rates, sales tax rates, district taxes, and total tax amount.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mt-2 flex flex-wrap gap-1\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",\\\"california tax calculator\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"california tax calculator\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",\\\"tax amount in california\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"tax amount in california\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",\\\"sales tax rate\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"sales tax rate\\\"}]]}]]}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L11\\\",\\\"texas-tax-guide-2026\\\",{\\\"href\\\":\\\"/blog/texas-tax-guide-2026\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"group rounded-xl border border-border/50 bg-card p-5 hover:border-emerald-500/30 hover:shadow-md transition-all\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"flex items-center gap-2 mb-2\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border px-2 py-0.5 text-[10px] font-medium bg-violet-100 text-violet-800 dark:bg-violet-500/20 dark:text-violet-400 border-violet-300 dark:border-violet-500/30\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"State Tax\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"text-[10px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"June 19, 2026\\\"}]]}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"h3\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"text-sm font-semibold text-foreground group-hover:text-emerald-600 dark:group-hover:text-emerald-400 transition-colors line-clamp-2 mb-1.5\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Texas Tax Guide 2026: No Income Tax, But What About Property and Sales Tax?\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"text-xs text-muted-foreground line-clamp-2\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Texas has no state income tax, but property tax is among the highest. Complete guide to Texas taxes in 2026.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mt-2 flex flex-wrap gap-1\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",\\\"texas tax calculator\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"texas tax calculator\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",\\\"income tax in texas\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"income tax in texas\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",\\\"taxable income\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"taxable income\\\"}]]}]]}],\\\"$L2a\\\"]}]]}]\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"2b:T450c,\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"\\u003cp\\u003eWashington state — the Evergreen State — is known for its booming tech industry, stunning natural beauty, and... no state income tax. Like Texas and Florida, Washington doesn't tax your personal income. But before you pack your bags, you should understand that Washington compensates with one of the highest sales tax rates in the country, plus a capital gains tax that catches some people by surprise.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eLet's break down everything you need to know about Washington state taxes in 2026.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"no-state-income-tax-in-washington-the-big-advantage\\\"\\u003eNo State Income Tax in Washington: The Big Advantage\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWashington is one of nine states with no personal income tax. This means:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eNo state tax on wages, salaries, or self-employment income\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eNo state tax on retirement income, Social Security, or pensions\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eNo state tax return to file (for personal income)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eNo state withholding from your paycheck\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eFor someone earning $100,000, this translates to savings of roughly $5,000–$8,000 compared to states like California or New York. The savings scale with income — a $200,000 earner could save $15,000+ per year.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eUse our \\u003ca href=\\\"/washington-tax-calculator\\\"\\u003eWashington Tax Calculator\\u003c/a\\u003e to see your exact take-home pay.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"washington-sales-tax-among-the-highest-in-the-nation\\\"\\u003eWashington Sales Tax: Among the Highest in the Nation\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eHere's the trade-off. Washington's state sales tax rate is 6.5%, and local sales taxes push the combined rate much higher. In Seattle, the combined rate is 10.25%, making it one of the highest in the United States.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"combined-sales-tax-rates-in-major-washington-cities\\\"\\u003eCombined Sales Tax Rates in Major Washington Cities\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cdiv class=\\\"table-wrapper\\\"\\u003e\\u003ctable\\u003e\\u003cthead\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eCity\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eCombined Sales Tax Rate\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/thead\\u003e\\u003ctbody\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eSeattle\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e10.25%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eTacoma\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e10.30%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eBellevue\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e10.10%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eSpokane\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e8.9%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eVancouver\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e8.7%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eOlympia\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e9.40%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/tbody\\u003e\\u003c/table\\u003e\\u003c/div\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"what-s-taxable-in-washington\\\"\\u003eWhat's Taxable in Washington?\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWashington's sales tax applies to most retail purchases, but there are important rules:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eGroceries\\u003c/strong\\u003e: Most unprepared food is exempt\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003ePrescription drugs\\u003c/strong\\u003e: Exempt\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eMotor vehicles\\u003c/strong\\u003e: Subject to a separate Motor Vehicle Sales/Use Tax\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eServices\\u003c/strong\\u003e: Many services are taxable in Washington (unlike many states)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThe Department of Revenue administers these taxes, and the rules can be complex. If you're running a business, you'll need to understand which of your products and services are subject to sales tax and at what rate.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"local-sales-taxes-explained\\\"\\u003eLocal Sales Taxes Explained\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eLocal sales taxes in Washington are approved by voters at the county and city level. This is why the rate varies so much across the state — each area has different voter-approved measures. The local sales taxes can add 0.5% to 3.5% on top of the state rate.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWhen you see a combined rate of 10.25% in Seattle, that breaks down to:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e6.5% state sales tax\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e3.75% local sales taxes (city + county + regional transit)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"property-tax-in-washington\\\"\\u003eProperty Tax in Washington\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWashington's average effective property tax rate is approximately 0.98%, which is below the national average of about 1.1%. This makes property taxes relatively affordable compared to states like Texas (1.60%) or New York (1.62%).\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"property-tax-by-home-value\\\"\\u003eProperty Tax by Home Value\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cdiv class=\\\"table-wrapper\\\"\\u003e\\u003ctable\\u003e\\u003cthead\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eHome Value\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eAnnual Property Tax (avg 0.98%)\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/thead\\u003e\\u003ctbody\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$400,000\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$3,920\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$600,000\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$5,880\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$800,000\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$7,840\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$1,000,000\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$9,800\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/tbody\\u003e\\u003c/table\\u003e\\u003c/div\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWashington also offers property tax exemptions for seniors (age 61+) and disabled veterans, which can significantly reduce the tax burden for qualifying residents.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"the-washington-capital-gains-tax\\\"\\u003eThe Washington Capital Gains Tax\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIn 2022, Washington introduced a 7% capital gains tax on long-term capital gains exceeding $262,000 (indexed for inflation). This is not an income tax — it specifically targets long-term capital gains from the sale of stocks, bonds, and other intangible assets.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eKey points about the capital gains tax:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eOnly applies to gains \\u003cstrong\\u003eabove\\u003c/strong\\u003e the threshold (~$262,000 for 2026)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eExemptions include real estate, retirement accounts, and agricultural assets\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eOnly affects high-income individuals with significant investment gains\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eThe tax is controversial and faces ongoing legal challenges\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eFor most Washington residents, this tax will never apply. But if you're selling a business or have large stock positions, it's something to plan for.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"business-and-occupation-b-o-tax\\\"\\u003eBusiness and Occupation (B\\u0026O) Tax\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWashington doesn't have a corporate income tax, but it does have the Business and Occupation (B\\u0026O) tax. This is a gross receipts tax, meaning it's calculated on your total revenue — not your profit. The rates vary by industry:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eService businesses\\u003c/strong\\u003e: 1.5% of gross revenue\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eRetailing\\u003c/strong\\u003e: 0.471% of gross revenue\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eManufacturing\\u003c/strong\\u003e: 0.484% of gross revenue\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eWholesaling\\u003c/strong\\u003e: 0.484% of gross revenue\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eSmall businesses with gross revenue under $125,000 may qualify for a B\\u0026O tax credit that eliminates or reduces the tax. The Department of Revenue handles all B\\u0026O tax administration.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"your-total-tax-burden-in-washington\\\"\\u003eYour Total Tax Burden in Washington\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eFor a single person earning $75,000 in Seattle with a $500,000 home:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eFederal income tax\\u003c/strong\\u003e: ~$11,414\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eFICA\\u003c/strong\\u003e: ~$5,738\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eWashington state income tax\\u003c/strong\\u003e: $0\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eProperty tax\\u003c/strong\\u003e: ~$4,900 (0.98% effective rate)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eSales tax (estimated)\\u003c/strong\\u003e: ~$3,000 (on $30,000 of taxable spending at 10.25%)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eTotal tax burden\\u003c/strong\\u003e: ~$25,052 (about 33.4% of gross income)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWait — that seems high for a \\\"no income tax\\\" state, right? The key difference is that in Washington, the tax burden falls heavily on consumption (sales tax) and property ownership rather than income. If you rent rather than own, your tax burden drops significantly.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"state-or-local-understanding-washington-s-tax-structure\\\"\\u003eState or Local: Understanding Washington's Tax Structure\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThe question of state or local taxes in Washington is interesting because the state relies heavily on local option taxes. Unlike states where the state income tax does the heavy lifting, Washington's system is decentralized:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003col\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eState level\\u003c/strong\\u003e: 6.5% sales tax + B\\u0026O tax + capital gains tax\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eLocal level\\u003c/strong\\u003e: Additional sales taxes (up to 3.75%) + property taxes\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eNo state income tax\\u003c/strong\\u003e: Residents keep 100% of their earned income from state taxation\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ol\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThis structure means that your tax burden in Washington depends heavily on where you live and how much you spend. Someone in Spokane (8.9% sales tax) pays significantly less than someone in Seattle (10.25%), even with the same income.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"comparing-washington-to-other-states\\\"\\u003eComparing Washington to Other States\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cdiv class=\\\"table-wrapper\\\"\\u003e\\u003ctable\\u003e\\u003cthead\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eTax Type\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eWashington\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eCalifornia\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eOregon\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/thead\\u003e\\u003ctbody\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eIncome Tax\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e0%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e1% – 13.3%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e4.75% – 9.9%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eSales Tax\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e6.5% – 10.25%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e7.25% – 10.5%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e0%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eProperty Tax\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e0.98%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e0.71%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e0.93%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eTotal Burden\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e~8.24%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e~11.49%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e~9.90%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/tbody\\u003e\\u003c/table\\u003e\\u003c/div\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWashington's total tax burden is lower than both California and Oregon, but the distribution is different. Washington taxes spending, not earning. Oregon is the opposite — no sales tax but high income tax. Use our \\u003ca href=\\\"/compare\\\"\\u003ecomparison tools\\u003c/a\\u003e to see exact numbers.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"is-washington-right-for-you\\\"\\u003eIs Washington Right for You?\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eWashington makes sense if:\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eYou have a high income and want to avoid state income tax\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eYou're a renter (you avoid property tax)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eYou're retiring and want to protect your retirement income\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eYou work in tech and earn stock (long-term holds may qualify for capital gains rates)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eWashington may not be ideal if:\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eYou spend a large portion of your income on taxable goods\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eYou own an expensive home (property tax + sales tax can add up)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eYou have significant capital gains above the $262,000 threshold\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eUse our \\u003ca href=\\\"/washington-tax-calculator\\\"\\u003eWashington Tax Calculator\\u003c/a\\u003e to see your complete tax picture, including sales tax analysis and property tax estimates.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"seattle-income-tax-and-sales-tax-what-you-ll-pay\\\"\\u003eSeattle Income Tax and Sales Tax: What You'll Pay\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"seattle-income-tax\\\"\\u003eSeattle Income Tax\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThere is no Seattle income tax. Washington state has no personal income tax, and Seattle does not impose any local income tax. The Seattle income tax rate for individuals is 0%. If you search for a Seattle income tax calculator, the result will show only federal income tax and FICA — no state or city income tax.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eHowever, Seattle does have a JumpStart tax on large businesses with highly compensated employees, which indirectly affects workers but doesn't appear as a personal income tax.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"seattle-sales-tax-2026\\\"\\u003eSeattle Sales Tax 2026\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eSeattle sales tax is 10.25% for 2026, making it one of the highest in the nation. This combined rate includes:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e6.5% Washington state sales tax\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e3.75% local Seattle + King County + Sound Transit taxes\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThe total sales tax in Seattle applies to most retail purchases, restaurant meals, and many services. Some items are exempt, including groceries and prescription drugs.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"bellevue-everett-and-vancouver-wa-sales-tax\\\"\\u003eBellevue, Everett, and Vancouver WA Sales Tax\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cdiv class=\\\"table-wrapper\\\"\\u003e\\u003ctable\\u003e\\u003cthead\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eCity\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eCombined Sales Tax Rate\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/thead\\u003e\\u003ctbody\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eSeattle\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e10.25%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eBellevue WA\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e10.10%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eEverett WA\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e10.40%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eTacoma (Pierce County)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e10.30%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eMarysville WA\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e9.30%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eVancouver WA\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e8.70%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/tbody\\u003e\\u003c/table\\u003e\\u003c/div\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eVancouver WA has the lowest sales tax among major Washington cities, benefiting from its proximity to tax-free Oregon.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"washington-income-tax-why-it-s-zero-and-will-it-change\\\"\\u003eWashington Income Tax: Why It's Zero and Will It Change?\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"washington-state-income-tax-rate\\\"\\u003eWashington State Income Tax Rate\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThe Washington state income tax rate is 0% for all individuals. The Washington income tax law has been challenged multiple times, but the state constitution requires a uniform property tax, and courts have ruled that income is property — meaning a graduated income tax would be unconstitutional.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"washington-income-tax-proposal-history\\\"\\u003eWashington Income Tax Proposal History\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThere have been several washington income tax proposal attempts:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e2010: Initiative 1098 (high-earner income tax) — rejected by voters 64% to 36%\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e2021: WA income tax on millionaires (capital gains tax) — passed, but only applies to long-term capital gains over ~$262,000\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e2023-2024: Further washington income tax on millionaires discussions, but no new individual income tax enacted\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThe washington income tax constitution makes implementing a progressive income tax extremely difficult. Any washington income tax brackets would require a constitutional amendment.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"wa-income-tax-2026\\\"\\u003eWA Income Tax 2026\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eFor WA income tax 2026, there is still no personal state income tax. The only \\\"income-like\\\" tax is the 7% capital gains tax on long-term gains exceeding approximately $262,000.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"washington-property-tax-rates-exemptions-and-lookup\\\"\\u003eWashington Property Tax: Rates, Exemptions, and Lookup\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"washington-property-tax-rate\\\"\\u003eWashington Property Tax Rate\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThe Washington property tax rate averages 0.98% of assessed home value, below the national average. However, rates vary significantly by county and city. Property tax in Seattle WA tends to be higher than the state average due to voter-approved levies.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"washington-property-tax-exemption-for-seniors-and-disabled-veterans\\\"\\u003eWashington Property Tax Exemption for Seniors and Disabled Veterans\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWashington offers property tax exemptions for:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eSeniors (61+)\\u003c/strong\\u003e: Exempts up to $60,000 of assessed value (or 60% for higher incomes)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eDisabled veterans\\u003c/strong\\u003e: 100% disabled veterans may qualify for total exemption\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eSurviving spouses\\u003c/strong\\u003e: Of qualifying seniors/veterans\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThe washington property tax exemption for seniors is income-based, with three tiers of benefits.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"washington-property-tax-lookup-and-payment\\\"\\u003eWashington Property Tax Lookup and Payment\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eYou can:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eSearch washington property tax records via your county assessor's website\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eUse washington property tax lookup tools by parcel number or address\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003ePay washington property tax online through county portals\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eCheck washington property tax due dates (typically April 30 and October 31)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"county-specific-property-tax\\\"\\u003eCounty-Specific Property Tax\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cdiv class=\\\"table-wrapper\\\"\\u003e\\u003ctable\\u003e\\u003cthead\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eCounty\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eAverage Effective Rate\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/thead\\u003e\\u003ctbody\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eKing County (Seattle)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e0.93%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003ePierce County (Tacoma)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e1.05%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eSnohomish County (Everett)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e1.04%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eClark County (Vancouver)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e1.08%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/tbody\\u003e\\u003c/table\\u003e\\u003c/div\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003ePierce County property tax and Snohomish County property tax tend to be slightly higher than King County. Use a washington property tax calculator for precise estimates.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"how-much-is-your-salary-after-taxes-in-washington\\\"\\u003eHow Much Is Your Salary After Taxes in Washington?\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"100-000-after-taxes-in-washington-state\\\"\\u003e$100,000 After Taxes in Washington State\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eOn $100,000 in Washington (single filer), your take-home pay is approximately \\u003cstrong\\u003e$76,800\\u003c/strong\\u003e:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eFederal income tax: ~$14,001\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eFICA: ~$7,649\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eWashington state income tax: $0\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eTotal tax\\u003c/strong\\u003e: ~$21,650 (21.7% effective rate)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"300-000-after-taxes-in-washington-state\\\"\\u003e$300,000 After Taxes in Washington State\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eOn $300,000 in Washington, your take-home pay is approximately \\u003cstrong\\u003e$210,000\\u003c/strong\\u003e. Without state income tax, Washington saves you roughly $20,000–$25,000 compared to California or New York at this income level.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWashington's lack of income tax makes it especially attractive for high earners. The more you make, the more you save compared to states with progressive income tax.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"frequently-asked-questions-about-washington-taxes\\\"\\u003eFrequently Asked Questions About Washington Taxes\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"is-washington-state-sales-tax-10-percent\\\"\\u003eIs Washington state sales tax 10 percent?\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWashington state sales tax can exceed 10% in some areas. The state base rate is 6.5%, but combined with local taxes, Seattle reaches 10.25%, and Everett reaches 10.40%. Not all of Washington is 10%+ — Vancouver WA is only 8.70%.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"why-is-wa-sales-tax-so-high\\\"\\u003eWhy is WA sales tax so high?\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWA sales tax is high because Washington has no personal income tax. The state relies on sales tax and property tax as its primary revenue sources instead. When voters approve local measures (like Sound Transit), rates increase further.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"why-does-washington-state-have-no-income-tax\\\"\\u003eWhy does Washington state have no income tax?\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWashington's constitution requires uniform property taxation, and courts have interpreted income as property — meaning a graduated income tax (with different rates) would be unconstitutional. Multiple attempts to introduce an income tax have been rejected by voters.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"is-washington-a-high-tax-state\\\"\\u003eIs Washington a high tax state?\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWashington's total tax burden (~8.24%) is actually below the national average (~9.8%). While sales tax is high, the absence of income tax means many residents pay less overall than in states like California (~11.49%) or New York (~12.7%).\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"how-much-is-the-property-tax-in-washington\\\"\\u003eHow much is the property tax in Washington?\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWashington's average effective property tax rate is 0.98% of assessed home value. On a $500,000 home, that's approximately $4,900 per year. Rates vary by county — King County averages 0.93%, while Pierce County averages 1.05%.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"does-washington-have-income-tax\\\"\\u003eDoes Washington have income tax?\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eNo. Washington is one of nine states with no personal income tax. There is no state income tax on wages, salaries, self-employment income, retirement income, or investments. The only income-related tax is the 7% capital gains tax on long-term gains over ~$262,000.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"28:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mx-auto max-w-3xl lg